:REM behavior timing|TH|REM behavior timing":["u18"],"c44-reference-rem|row:REM behavior timing|TD|REM sleep behavior disorder usually appears at least 90 minutes after sleep onset.":["u19"],"copy|row:Periodic movements|TH|Periodic movements":["u20"],"c44-reference-movements|row:Periodic movements|TD|Periodic leg movements in sleep last between 0.5 seconds and 10 seconds. The period between periodic leg movements in sleep is 5 to 90 seconds.":["u21"],"copy|row:Insomnia duration|TH|Insomnia duration":["u22"],"c44-reference-insomnia|row:Insomnia duration|TD|Insomnia is categorized as chronic if it persists more than 3 months. Insomnia is categorized as short term if it has lasted fewer than 3 months.":["u23"],"copy|CHAPTER 44 / HAZZARD":["u24"],"c44-opening|Read each disorder with its own definition, diagnostic test and treatment evidence.":["u25"],"c44-opening|Keep similar numbers attached to the symptom, measurement and population they describe.":["u26"],"c44-burden|More than two-thirds of older people with multiple comorbidities report sleep problems.":["u27"],"c44-comorbidity|Poor sleep is common in older adults and is more closely associated with comorbidities than with chronological age.":["u28"],"c44-cycle|Each sleep period involves a progression of sleep stages that tend to cycle every 90 minutes.":["u29"],"c44-architecture|N3 sleep is also known as slow wave sleep.":["u30"],"c44-architecture|Sleep efficiency is the amount of time actually asleep divided by time spent in bed.":["u31"],"c44-architecture|The reduction in N3 sleep with age is more prominent in men.":["u32"],"c44-sdb-cohort|A 2007–2010 cohort of middle-aged adults estimated moderate to severe sleep-disordered breathing in 6% of women and 13% of men.":["u282"],"c44-sdb-cohort|These estimates describe that middle-aged cohort, not an older-adult prevalence estimate.":["u283"],"c44-sdb-age-pattern|OSA is underdiagnosed, especially in women.":["u284"],"c44-sdb-age-pattern|Most studies find that risk increases with age until about 70, then plateaus.":["u285"],"c44-sdb-age-pattern|Premenopausal status appears protective, so the sex difference narrows in older populations.":["u286"],"c44-sdb-population-severity|The chapter reports that OSA may be more severe in African-American and Asian populations than in Caucasian populations.":["u287"],"c44-airflow-duration|Sleep-disordered breathing is characterized by disturbed respiration during sleep arising from repetitive events of complete or partial cessation of airflow lasting at least 10 seconds.":["u33"],"c44-effort|Obstructive sleep apnea is determined by the persistence of respiratory effort during cessation of airflow.":["u34"],"c44-effort|Central sleep apnea is characterized by a concomitant cessation of breathing effort during cessation of airflow.":["u35"],"c44-central-cause|Heart failure is the most commonly recognized cause of central sleep apnea.":["u36"],"c44-ahi-diagnosis|Sleep-disordered breathing can be diagnosed when the apnea hypopnea index is greater than 15.":["u37"],"c44-ahi-diagnosis|Sleep-disordered breathing can be diagnosed when the apnea hypopnea index is greater than 5 with significant symptoms or related comorbidities.":["u38"],"c44-ahi-severe|An apnea hypopnea index greater than 30 is generally considered to indicate severe sleep-disordered breathing.":["u39"],"c44-presentation|The paradigmatic patient with sleep-disordered breathing is an obese male with nocturnal snoring, witnessed apneas, frequent nocturnal awakenings, and excessive daytime sleepiness.":["u40"],"c44-presentation|In older adults, an elevated body mass index has less predictive value of the likelihood of obstructive sleep apnea.":["u41"],"c44-neck|A neck circumference greater than 17 inches in men is a predictor for sleep-disordered breathing on physical examination.":["u42"],"c44-neck|A neck circumference greater than 16 inches in women is a predictor for sleep-disordered breathing on physical examination.":["u43"],"c44-heart-failure|Upwards of 50% of patients with stable heart failure have some form of sleep-disordered breathing.":["u44"],"c44-breathing-tests|An attended in-laboratory polysomnography is considered the gold standard test for sleep-disordered breathing.":["u45"],"c44-breathing-tests|Home sleep apnea tests are not validated for the measurement of central apneas.":["u46"],"c44-breathing-tests|A negative home sleep apnea test does not rule out obstructive sleep apnea.":["u47"],"c44-cpap-dementia|Mild to moderate dementia is not a contraindication to continuous positive airway pressure initiation.":["u48"],"c44-cpap-pressure|Continuous positive airway pressure traditionally delivers air pressure between 5 and 20 cm H2O.":["u49"],"c44-cpap-adherence|At the end of a year only 50% to 70% of all adult patients are adherent to continuous positive airway pressure therapy.":["u50"],"c44-asv|Adaptive servo-ventilation is generally not recommended in those with an ejection fraction less than 45%.":["u51"],"c44-mandibular-device|Mandibular advancement devices are generally thought to be more effective in mild to moderate obstructive sleep apnea rather than severe obstructive sleep apnea.":["u52"],"c44-mandibular-device|Teeth are generally required to anchor a mandibular advancement device in place.":["u53"],"c44-hypoglossal-mechanism|Hypoglossal nerve stimulation uses a lead positioned at the respiratory muscles to sense inspiration and a second lead at the hypoglossal nerve.":["u307"],"c44-hypoglossal-mechanism|Inspiration triggers tongue contraction, opening the airway both directly and through muscular coupling with the soft palate.":["u308"],"c44-hypoglossal-trial-scope|The chapter describes promising randomised-trial results in a highly selected group unable to tolerate CPAP and with BMI below 32 kg/m².":["u309"],"c44-hypoglossal-trial-scope|Older adults were sparsely represented in the initial trials.":["u310"],"c44-hypoglossal-trial-scope|These are the chapter’s study-selection criteria, not a current universal eligibility rule.":["u311"],"c44-hypoglossal-older-cohort|A cohort cited in the chapter included 62 patients aged 65–80 and found significant reductions in apnoea–hypopnoea index and sleepiness scores.":["u312"],"c44-hypoglossal-older-cohort|This supports benefit in that selected older cohort; it does not establish benefit for every older patient.":["u313"],"c44-hypoglossal-current-selection|FDA scope update: the June 8, 2023 Inspire approval increased the recommended BMI upper limit to 40.":["u314"],"c44-hypoglossal-current-selection|PAP failure or intolerance and the absence of complete concentric soft-palate collapse remain important selection requirements.":["u315"],"c44-hypoglossal-current-selection|Device-specific assessment is still required.":["u316"],"c44-sdb-surgery|The chapter identifies uvulopalatopharyngoplasty (UPPP) as the most common operation for sleep-disordered breathing.":["u317"],"c44-sdb-surgery|Success varies with anatomy and obesity; disease may recur after initial success because of scar-tissue growth.":["u318"],"c44-sdb-surgery|Maxillomandibular advancement is a more aggressive alternative.":["u319"],"c44-rls-definition|Restless legs syndrome is characterized by the urge to move one's legs that improves with movement, worsens with relaxation, and occurs later in the day.":["u54"],"c44-rls-testing|There is no objective test to diagnose restless legs syndrome.":["u55"],"c44-rls-testing|The diagnosis of restless legs syndrome can be based on clinical criteria alone.":["u56"],"c44-rls-prevalence|Restless legs syndrome is estimated to affect up to 8% of older adults.":["u57"],"c44-rls-drugs|Taking antihistamines and most antidepressants are risk factors for the development of restless legs syndrome.":["u58"],"c44-rls-drugs|Bupropion is less frequently associated with restless legs syndrome than selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors.":["u59"],"c44-rls-antidepressant-premise|Most antidepressants can elicit or provoke restless legs symptoms; bupropion is less frequently associated with RLS.":["u329"],"c44-rls-antidepressant-premise|This difference is the reason for the chapter’s medication-switch suggestion.":["u330"],"c44-rls-switch-conclusion|When RLS symptoms are present in a patient taking an SSRI or SNRI, a switch to bupropion is a reasonable option to consider.":["u331"],"c44-rls-switch-conclusion|This is a relative association and a treatment-review option, not a claim that bupropion can never provoke symptoms or that every patient must switch.":["u332"],"c44-movement-guide|Keep the movement pattern, the count recorded during sleep and the disorder diagnosis in separate notes.":["u60"],"c44-movement-guide|They answer different questions.":["u61"],"c44-movement-duration|Periodic leg movements in sleep last between 0.5 seconds and 10 seconds.":["u62"],"c44-movement-duration|The period between periodic leg movements in sleep is 5 to 90 seconds.":["u63"],"c44-movement-duration|At least four movements in a row must occur to count as periodic leg movements in sleep.":["u64"],"c44-movement-threshold|Current AASM criteria require more than 15 periodic limb movements per hour in adults, together with clinically significant sleep disturbance or daytime dysfunction not better explained by another disorder.":["u320"],"c44-movement-threshold|The movement count alone is insufficient.":["u321"],"c44-movement-study|A sleep study with limb electrode measurement is required for the diagnosis of periodic limb movement disorder.":["u66"],"c44-movement-prevalence|Periodic leg movements in sleep are seen on polysomnography in up to 45% of older people.":["u67"],"c44-movement-prevalence|Eighty percent to 90% of patients with restless legs syndrome have periodic leg movements in sleep.":["u68"],"c44-plms-comparison-intro|The chapter’s prevalence estimates explain why polysomnographic limb movements must be interpreted in their diagnostic context.":["u322"],"c44-plms-comparison-intro|The percentages below describe PLMS within each named disorder.":["u323"],"c44-plmd-osa-exclusion|The AASM criteria also exclude untreated obstructive sleep apnoea and require clinically significant symptoms attributable to the movements.":["u324"],"c44-plmd-osa-exclusion|An incidental PLMS finding is not sufficient for PLMD.":["u325"],"c44-hypnic-jerks|Hypnic jerks are total body jerking often with a sensation of falling that occurs at sleep onset.":["u69"],"c44-iron|Patients should receive iron supplementation if the serum ferritin is less than 75 mcg/L.":["u70"],"c44-iron|Intravenous iron is suggested for those with moderate to severe restless legs syndrome and a ferritin less than 300 mcg/L.":["u71"],"c44-dopamine-timing|Long-acting dopamine agonists for restless legs syndrome should be taken approximately 2 hours before bedtime.":["u72"],"c44-augmentation-definition|Augmentation is a worsening of restless legs syndrome manifested by earlier onset, increased intensity, or spread of symptoms to the upper extremities and trunk.":["u73"],"c44-augmentation-comparison|Augmentation is much more frequent with carbidopa-levodopa than with long-acting dopamine agonists.":["u74"],"c44-augmentation-rate|Estimates of augmentation on long-acting dopamine agonists range between 2% and 15% per year.":["u75"],"c44-rbd-definition|REM sleep behavior disorder is defined as recurrent complex movements or vocalizations during sleep characterized by dream enactment.":["u76"],"c44-rbd-definition|REM sleep without atonia is required for the diagnosis of REM sleep behavior disorder.":["u77"],"c44-rbd-age|REM sleep behavior disorder is a male-predominant syndrome which usually occurs after the age of 50.":["u78"],"c44-rbd-prognosis|Up to 90% of patients who present with REM sleep behavior disorder develop another synucleinopathy disorder over a 10- to 15-year follow-up.":["u79"],"c44-rbd-timing|REM sleep behavior disorder usually appears at least 90 minutes after sleep onset.":["u80"],"c44-rbd-test-treatment|An attended in-laboratory polysomnography is the gold standard for diagnosing REM sleep behavior disorder.":["u81"],"c44-rbd-test-treatment|Melatonin is the primary first choice for pharmacologic therapy of REM sleep behavior disorder in most circumstances.":["u82"],"c44-melatonin-regulation-premise|The chapter’s concern is that US melatonin supplements are not regulated as pharmaceutical drugs.":["u333"],"c44-melatonin-regulation-premise|More precisely, melatonin is regulated as a dietary supplement, with less stringent requirements than prescription or over-the-counter drugs; dietary supplements do not undergo FDA premarket approval for safety and effectiveness.":["u334"],"c44-melatonin-quality-conclusion|Consequently, do not assume pharmaceutical-level consistency of melatonin strength and quality: a supplement’s content may differ from its label.":["u335"],"c44-melatonin-quality-conclusion|This does not mean that supplements are wholly outside FDA regulation.":["u336"],"c44-rbd-bupropion|Bupropion is an antidepressant that is not recognized as a precipitating factor for REM sleep behavior disorder symptoms.":["u83"],"c44-rbd-narcolepsy|RBD is strongly associated with narcolepsy, possibly reflecting another form of REM motor-behaviour dysregulation.":["u326"],"c44-rbd-narcolepsy|SSRIs used to treat cataplexy may worsen RBD.":["u327"],"c44-rbd-neurologic-associations|Other neurologic disorders associated with RBD include cerebrovascular disease, multiple sclerosis, Guillain–Barré syndrome, normal-pressure hydrocephalus and Tourette syndrome.":["u328"],"c44-nonrem|Non-REM parasomnias such as sleep walking, confusional arousals, and sleep terrors predominantly occur in the first part of the night.":["u84"],"c44-phase-definitions|Delayed sleep-wake phase disorder is a delay in the phase of the endogenous major sleep episode with respect to the desired major sleep episode.":["u85"],"c44-phase-definitions|Advanced sleep-wake phase disorder is an advance in the phase of the endogenous major sleep episode with respect to the desired major sleep episode.":["u86"],"c44-irregular-definition|Irregular sleep-wake rhythm disorder is characterized by a lack of a clearly defined circadian rhythm resulting in insomnia at night and excessive sleep during the day.":["u87"],"c44-non24|Non-24-hour sleep-wake rhythm disorder usually occurs from a patient's inability to entrain their circadian rhythm to light due to complete blindness.":["u88"],"c44-irregular-duration|Patients with irregular sleep-wake rhythm disorder will generally sleep for less than 4 hours at a time.":["u89"],"c44-sleep-log|Sleep logs for 1 to 2 weeks are helpful in establishing the diagnosis of a circadian disorder.":["u90"],"c44-light-timing|Exposure to bright light in the morning will advance the circadian rhythm.":["u91"],"c44-light-timing|Exposure to bright light in the evening will cause a delay in the circadian rhythm.":["u92"],"c44-insomnia-duration|Insomnia is categorized as chronic if it persists more than 3 months.":["u93"],"c44-insomnia-duration|Insomnia is categorized as short term if it has lasted fewer than 3 months.":["u94"],"c44-insomnia-prevalence|Insomnia affects about 30% to 48% of those older than 65 years.":["u95"],"c44-insomnia-testing|Polysomnography and other sleep studies are not necessary in the evaluation of insomnia unless a comorbid sleep condition is suspected.":["u96"],"c44-cbti|Cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia is recommended as the first-line treatment for all insomnia in adults.":["u97"],"c44-hygiene|Sleep hygiene is not generally effective when used alone for chronic insomnia.":["u98"],"c44-stimulus-control|In stimulus-control therapy, if the patient cannot fall asleep within approximately 20 minutes they should get out of bed and do a relaxing activity.":["u99"],"c44-restriction|In sleep-restriction therapy, the patient is only allowed to spend their calculated actual sleep time plus around 15 minutes in bed each night for the following week.":["u100"],"c44-medication-guide|Read the reported effects together with the cautions that follow.":["u101"],"c44-medication-guide|An effect estimate is not a complete prescribing plan.":["u102"],"c44-benzodiazepine-class-safety|The chapter describes benzodiazepine adverse effects including confusion, falls, rebound insomnia, tolerance and withdrawal; it notes an association with pneumonia in Alzheimer disease and that these drugs are on the Beers list.":["u251"],"c44-nbra-class-safety|The nonbenzodiazepine receptor agonists bind selectively to GABA-A, causing sedation and amnesia without anxiolysis.":["u252"],"c44-nbra-class-safety|The chapter describes efficacy similar to benzodiazepines with a somewhat better adverse-effect profile, but also places these agents on the Beers list.":["u253"],"c44-nbra-class-safety|Complex sleep behaviours, including sleep driving and sleep eating, have occurred with zolpidem and may be a class effect.":["u254"],"c44-nbra-duration-context|Zolpidem and zaleplon should be taken immediately before bed because of rapid onset.":["u255"],"c44-nbra-duration-context|Among zolpidem, zaleplon and eszopiclone, eszopiclone has the longest duration and can help sleep maintenance but may cause morning drowsiness.":["u256"],"c44-nbra-duration-context|Extended-release zolpidem is also used for maintenance insomnia.":["u257"],"c44-ramelteon-mechanism|Ramelteon acts selectively at MT1/MT2 receptors rather than GABA receptors; the chapter reports reduced sleep latency and increased sleep time in older adults with fewer adverse effects.":["u258"],"c44-hypnotic-prescribing-rule|When a sedative-hypnotic is used in an older person, choose the smallest dose of the agent with the least adverse-event risk for the shortest necessary duration.":["u259"],"c44-hypnotic-prescribing-rule|The chapter distinguishes short-acting agents for difficulty falling asleep from intermediate-acting agents for difficulty staying asleep.":["u260"],"c44-zolpidem-half-life|The supplied reviewer interprets zolpidem’s half-life as 1.5–4.5 hours, with 3 hours in older adults and 10 hours in hepatic cirrhosis.":["u288"],"c44-zolpidem-half-life|OCR dropped a leading digit; the reviewer chose this reading for consistency with the 3-hour value in the same cell.":["u289"],"c44-zolpidem-half-life|This interpretation is recorded for review, not certified verbatim.":["u290"],"c44-half-life-read-variance|The publisher text layer gives doxepin’s half-life as 15.3 hours and its metabolite’s as 31 hours; this resolves the earlier doxepin OCR variance.":["u345"],"c44-half-life-read-variance|The accepted temazepam half-life remains 8.8 hours (read twice; 8.4 on one pass).":["u346"],"c44-suvorexant-start|As printed in TABLE 44-5, suvorexant has an initial dose of 5 mg, a dose range of 10–20 mg and a half-life of 12 hours.":["u347"],"c44-suvorexant-start|Compare the separate prescribing-label dose guidance below.":["u348"],"c44-suvorexant-book-approval|Hazzard states that suvorexant is the only medication specifically approved for insomnia among those with Alzheimer dementia.":["u349"],"c44-suvorexant-book-approval|The FDA-label comparison below distinguishes the indication from the Alzheimer trial evidence.":["u350"],"c44-suvorexant-book-trial|Hazzard’s supplied account describes a relatively small trial of 277 participants with mild to moderate Alzheimer dementia and insomnia, with around half an hour more sleep after 2 weeks and few adverse effects.":["u351"],"c44-suvorexant-book-trial|Its exact book page and these trial details remain unconfirmed; compare the original trial below.":["u352"],"c44-suvorexant-label-comparison|The March 2025 FDA label indicates suvorexant for insomnia with difficulty initiating or maintaining sleep.":["u353"],"c44-suvorexant-label-comparison|It includes Alzheimer-dementia trial data but does not state a separate Alzheimer-specific indication or establish exclusivity among treatments.":["u354"],"c44-suvorexant-label-dose|FDA label dose: usually 10 mg once nightly, increased to a maximum of 20 mg if tolerated but ineffective.":["u355"],"c44-suvorexant-label-dose|With moderate CYP3A inhibitors, use 5 mg and generally do not exceed 10 mg.":["u356"],"c44-suvorexant-label-dose|The book’s 5 mg initial-dose cell is retained as its own recommendation, not substituted for these label instructions.":["u357"],"c44-suvorexant-ad-trial|In the original four-week, double-blind trial, 285 patients with mild-to-moderate probable Alzheimer dementia and insomnia were randomized: 142 to suvorexant and 143 to placebo; 277 completed it.":["u358"],"c44-suvorexant-ad-trial|Suvorexant was started at 10 mg and could be increased to 20 mg.":["u359"],"c44-suvorexant-ad-result|At week 4, total sleep time increased by 73 minutes with suvorexant versus 45 minutes with placebo: a between-group difference of 28 minutes (95% CI 11–45; p<0.01).":["u360"],"c44-suvorexant-ad-result|Somnolence occurred in 4.2% versus 1.4%.":["u361"],"c44-suvorexant-ad-result|Thus 277 is the number completing the trial, and this primary efficacy result is at week 4; it does not verify the book’s two-week statement.":["u362"],"c44-benzodiazepine-effects|Benzodiazepines decrease the time to fall asleep by about 10 minutes on average.":["u103"],"c44-benzodiazepine-effects|Benzodiazepines increase the total sleep time by 30 to 60 minutes during the nighttime.":["u104"],"c44-benzodiazepine-caution|Flurazepam and quazepam should not be used in older adults due to next-day effects.":["u105"],"c44-zdrug-timing|Zolpidem and zaleplon should only be taken immediately before bed because of their rapid onset of action.":["u106"],"c44-doxepin|TABLE 44-5 gives doxepin an initial dose of 3 mg and a range of 3–6 mg.":["u363"],"c44-doxepin|It should not be taken within 3 hours of a meal.":["u364"],"c44-sedatives-cautions|Sedating antipsychotics should not routinely be used in the management of insomnia in older adults without coexisting serious psychiatric conditions.":["u108"],"c44-sedatives-cautions|Diphenhydramine should not be used as a primary treatment for insomnia in older patients.":["u109"],"c44-sedatives-cautions|Kava has a significant risk of hepatotoxicity and is not recommended for insomnia.":["u110"],"c44-dementia-caution|Most pharmacologic therapies for insomnia are relatively contraindicated in those with significant dementia.":["u111"],"c44-reference-guide|This table repeats selected findings to make their referents explicit.":["u112"],"c44-reference-guide|Keep the full sentence when recalling a threshold.":["u113"],"copy|Figure 44-2":["u384","u608","u1276"],"copy|Martínez-García M, Chiner E, Hernández L, et al. Obstructive sleep apnoea in the elderly: role of":["u543"],"copy|continuous positive airway pressure treatment. Eur Respir J . 2015;46(1):142–151.":["u543"],"copy|McCurry SM, Pike KC, Vitiello MV, et al. Increasing walking and bright light exposure to improve sleep in community-dwelling persons with Alzheimer’s disease: results of a randomized, controlled trial. J Am Geriatr Soc . 2011;59(8):1393–1402.":["u544"],"copy|McMillan A, Bratton DJ, Faria R, et al. Continuous positive airway pressure in older people with obstructive sleep apnoea syndrome (PREDICT): a 12-month, multicentre, randomised trial. Lancet Respir Med . 2014;2(10): 804–812.":["u544"],"copy|Posadas T, Oscullo G, Zaldívar E, et al. Treatment with CPAP in elderly patients with obstructive sleep apnoea. J Clin Med . 2020;9(2):546.":["u544"],"copy|Qaseem A, Kansagara D, Forciea MA, Cooke M, Denberg TD. Clinical Guidelines Committee of the American College of Physicians. Management of Chronic Insomnia Disorder in Adults: A Clinical Practice Guideline From the American College of Physicians. Ann Intern Med . 2016;165(2):125-133.":["u544"],"copy|Raggi A, Ferri R. Sleep disorders in neurodegenerative diseases. Eur J Neurol . 2010;17:1326–1338.":["u544"],"copy|Ramar K, Dort LC, Katz SG, et al. Clinical practice guideline for the treatment of obstructive sleep apnea and snoring with oral appliance therapy: an update for 2015. J Clin Sleep Med . 2015;11(7):773–827.":["u544"],"copy|On the other hand, if there is concomitant cessation of breathing effort, the disorder is classified as central sleep apnea (CSA) because there is a momentary defect in the central control of breathing":["u607"],"copy|(":["u607"],"copy|).":["u607"],"copy|.":["u608"],"copy|Thus, it is recommended that maximal":["u906"],"copy|TABLE 44-2 ■ PHARMACOLOGIC TREATMENT OF PLMS/RLS":["u906"]},"sequenceOffsets":[[384,1],[386,902],[387,0],[390,899],[391,-1],[392,898],[393,-2],[398,893],[399,-3],[498,794],[500,-5],[548,-3],[610,4],[611,-2],[616,-1],[627,7],[628,-2],[636,-1],[657,7],[658,-2],[666,-1],[679,6],[680,-2],[687,-1],[711,6],[712,-2],[719,-1],[907,1],[908,-1],[909,0],[968,6],[969,-1],[975,0],[1129,2],[1130,-1],[1132,0],[1161,1],[1162,-1],[1163,0],[1230,1],[1231,-1],[1232,3],[1237,-5],[1238,2],[1244,-11],[1245,1],[1275,2]],"preservedBreaks":{"Martínez-García M, Chiner E, Hernández L, et al. Obstructive sleep apnoea in the elderly: role of continuous positive airway pressure treatment. Eur Respir J . 2015;46(1):142–151.":[97],"On the other hand, if there is concomitant cessation of breathing effort, the disorder is classified as central sleep apnea (CSA) because there is a momentary defect in the central control of breathing ( Figure 44-2 ).":[201,203,215],"FIGURE 44-2.":[11],"Estimates of the prevalence of OSA have varied widely and are dependent on the populations studied.":[39],"Furthermore, premenopausal status appears to protect against OSA meaning that the gender discrepancy between men and women narrows in the older population.":[116],"Significant epidemiologic associations of OSA with adverse cardiovascular and metabolic consequences have been reported.":[45],"It is often helpful to obtain corroborating information from a bed partner regarding snoring, witnessed apneas as well as additional features that may point to a comorbid sleep disorder.":[118],"It can be difficult to tease apart symptoms of heart failure such as orthopnea and paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea (PND) from those of SDB.":[22],"Alternative testing includes the inference of respiratory events from peripheral arterial tone changes due to changes in sympathetic activity resulting from the respiratory events that can be correlated with pulse oximetry.":[80],"Modafinil and armodafinil, central nervous system stimulants, have Food and Drug Administration (FDA) approval for the treatment of residual excessive daytime sleepiness in treated OSA.":[49],"A PSG is sometimes helpful to rule out other sleep disorders, particularly SDB which may be a comorbid cause of sleep disturbance.":[50],"Thus, it is recommended that maximal doses recommended for RLS are not exceeded.":[36],"There are novel devices available for the treatment of RLS; however, the data available are extremely limited.":[87],"A detailed neurologic examination can elucidate a comorbid neurodegenerative disorder.":[21],"However, access to highly skilled professionals trained in such interventions may be limited in some health care systems.":[33],"Ramelteon has been shown to reduce total sleep latency and increase sleep time in older adults, with fewer side effects.":[106],"However, the results of studies have been mixed.":[41]}}
